\title{cgrc.bayes: Bayesian Implementation of the Correct Guess Rate Curve}
\description{
When participants in a placebo-controlled trial can tell which arm they are
in, expectancy can inflate the treatment contrast. The Correct Guess Rate
Curve (CGRC) of Szigeti et al. (2023) reweights the participants a trial
already has to show what the contrast would look like at a different
correct-guess rate, usually 0.50 (guessing at chance). This package
reproduces that estimand and reports its uncertainty as a posterior
distribution.
}
\section{Main functions}{

\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=cgrc]{cgrc()}} adjusts a trial; \code{\link[=cgrc_headline]{cgrc_headline()}} summarises it in two
probabilities.
\item \code{\link[=cgr_operating]{cgr_operating()}} checks, by simulation, whether CGR adjustment is reliable
for a trial design.
\item \code{\link[=cgrc_unknown]{cgrc_unknown()}} analyses trials whose guess question allowed
"I don't know".
\item \code{\link[=cgr_rope]{cgr_rope()}} splits the adjusted posterior into harm, negligible and
benefit.
\item \code{\link[=cgrc_app]{cgrc_app()}} opens the interactive design and analysis app.
}
}

